# Mutation cases for "a case the casefile no longer declares is not open work".
#
# Run with `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/a_hole_the_casefile_no_longer_declares.cases`.
#
# The defect was a debt list that named repaid debts. A ledger record is a
# reading of a casefile AS IT WAS, and `holes` walked the newest record without
# ever asking whether the cases in it still existed. Measured 2026-08-30: the
# corpus reported seven holes and FOUR of them were gone —
# `lua_engine_reads_ecmascript` had been judged holding eight cases, retiring
# the rewriter cut it to three, and the four survivors moved with the member
# surface to `the_frontend_now_owns_the_member_surface`, where a cargo round
# caught every one. `stale` knew that casefile had moved and said so in its own
# subcommand; nothing carried the fact into this one.
#
# The repair has two edges and both are here, because they fail in opposite
# directions and only one of them is loud. Reporting too FEW vanished cases
# leaves repaid debts on the list, which is the defect. Reporting too MANY
# empties the list while the debts stand — and that one is silent, so the
# guards that keep an unreadable label from reading as an absent case get two
# of the five cases below.
#
# `scripts/mutation-ledger` is a RUNTIME target: the tests drive it as a
# subprocess, so it is read fresh on every run rather than linked into the test
# binary, and the evidence a mutation reached what ran is the file differing on
# disk while the tests ran.
#
# Anchors are literal slices of the source; an edit that moves one is reported
# INCONCLUSIVE with the anchor quoted, never as a pass.

# ⚠ `ledger_holes_are_still_declared` and NOT `mutation_ledger`, which holds
# the rest of this tool's contract. That suite cannot be a baseline: one of its
# tests drives `scripts/mutate --check`, which calls `cargo metadata`, and
# inside a cargo round that comes back `cargo metadata failed — the selector
# cannot be checked` (measured 2026-08-30, through the run diagnostics the
# baseline refusal now prints). A suite that cannot be a baseline cannot be an
# oracle, which is why the contract these cases hold was given a file of its
# own.
mutation_tests -p sce-build --test ledger_holes_are_still_declared
mutation_runtime_targets scripts/mutation-ledger

# ── The defect itself ──────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Never ask whether the case is still there, and every stale record's holes
# come back as open work.

mutation_case "a hole is never asked whether its case still exists" <<'PY'
edit("scripts/mutation-ledger",
     """    if any(l == "" for l in entry["labels"]):
        return None
    return label in entry["labels"]""",
     """    if any(l == "" for l in entry["labels"]):
        return None
    return True""")
PY

# ── The silent direction: an unreadable label reads as an absent case ──
#
# A parser that drifted off the casefile format would report EVERY hole in the
# corpus as vanished, and the debt list would empty itself while the debts
# stood. Two guards hold that shut and each one is attacked below — a third
# stood here until the ⚠ note beneath them removed it as dead.

mutation_case "an unreadable label is treated as a case that is gone" <<'PY'
edit("scripts/mutation-ledger",
     """    if any(l == "" for l in entry["labels"]):
        return None
""",
     "")
PY

# ⚠ A sibling case stood here — "a label list that disagrees with the count is
# trusted" — and it SURVIVED, along with the one below it, because the tree
# carried TWO guards over the same ground: a length check against the case
# count, and the `int(cases)` test in the line this case edits. Each covered
# the other, so removing either changed nothing observable. The length check
# was the dead one and is gone; the survivors are how that was found.

mutation_case "an empty label list and an unreadable label are the same thing" <<'PY'
edit("scripts/mutation-ledger",
     """        label_list = labels.split("\\x1f") if int(cases) else []""",
     """        label_list = labels.split("\\x1f") if labels else []""")
PY

# ── The reader is left to find the new home alone ──────────────────
#
# A label another casefile declares is a case that MOVED, and saying so is what
# turns "this hole vanished" from something to investigate into something
# already answered.

mutation_case "a moved case is not followed to its new casefile" <<'PY'
edit("scripts/mutation-ledger",
     """                elsewhere = sorted(label_home.get(case["label"], set())
                                   - {entry["stem"]})""",
     """                elsewhere = []""")
PY

# ── The summary folds the two kinds of work back together ──────────
#
# An open case needs a repair; a vanished one needs a round. One number holding
# both says neither, which is the state this whole repair started from.

mutation_case "the summary counts a vanished case as open work" <<'PY'
edit("scripts/mutation-ledger",
     """                  if case["verdict"] != "CAUGHT"
                  and case_is_still_declared(e, case["label"]) is not False]""",
     """                  if case["verdict"] != "CAUGHT"]""")
PY
